Additional context

On GCC 7.3 + CUDA 10.0, and probably other configurations, we get lots of warnings like:
./c10/core/Backend.h(109): warning: missing return statement at end of non-void function "c10::tensorTypeIdToBackend"

./c10/core/Backend.h(173): warning: missing return statement at end of non-void function "c10::backendToDeviceType"

./c10/core/Backend.h(204): warning: missing return statement at end of non-void function "c10::backendToCPU"

./c10/core/Backend.h(226): warning: missing return statement at end of non-void function "c10::backendToCUDA"

./c10/core/Backend.h(245): warning: missing return statement at end of non-void function "c10::backendToHIP"

This is because nvcc is unable to deal with the combination of C10_UNLIKELY (aka __builtin_expect) and dead-code elimination, as seen in this simple program:
(/scratch/ezyang/pytorch-tmp-env) ezyang@devfair040:/scratch/ezyang/pytorch-tmp$ cat test.cu
#include <exception>
int f() {
  if (__builtin_expect(true, 0)) {
    throw std::exception();
  }
}
int g() {
  if (true) {
    throw std::exception();
  }
}
(/scratch/ezyang/pytorch-tmp-env) ezyang@devfair040:/scratch/ezyang/pytorch-tmp$ nvcc -c test.cu 
test.cu(6): warning: missing return statement at end of non-void function "f"

test.cu(6): warning: missing return statement at end of non-void function "f"


This combination of features is handled correctly by the host compiler. Bug was reported to NVIDIA at https://developer.nvidia.com/nvidia_bug/2615657

The extensions generated by function_wrapper.py use the types and argument names, not the full schema string (schema_args in create_extension_backend ignores is_nullable, should probably use full schema string). This means that optional arguments aren't properly set/handled.
E.g., MSNPUType.cpp generates a function:
Tensor MSNPUType::norm(const Tensor & self, c10::optional<Scalar> p, ScalarType dtype) const {
    return MSNPUTypeDispatch::get_function<Tensor (*)(const Tensor &, c10::optional<Scalar>, ScalarType)>("norm(Tensor self, Scalar p, ScalarType dtype) -> Tensor")(self, p, dtype);
}

Since p is optional, the generated schema string should be something like Scalar? p or optional<Scalar> p, not simply Scalar p. This can result in parameters set to invalid values (eg outside the range of a valid enumeration).
To test, we should be able to register a function with an optional argument in the tests in msnpu_extensions.cpp, run with python run_test.py -i cpp_extensions
This issue arose when updating msnpu_extension tests in:
#21088
